In the producer-consumer solution with N = 1, the producer's first two waits are swapped:
```
Producer: wait(mutex); wait(empty); add item; signal(full); signal(mutex);
Consumer: wait(full); wait(mutex); remove item; signal(mutex); signal(empty);
```
What can go wrong?

A reader-preference readers-writers solution uses semaphores mutex (for readcount) and wrt (for the data):
```
Reader:  wait(mutex); readcount++; if (readcount == 1) X; signal(mutex);
         ... read ...
         wait(mutex); readcount--; if (readcount == 0) Y; signal(mutex);
Writer:  wait(wrt); ... write ...; signal(wrt);
```
What are X and Y?

Three threads share semaphores X, Y and Z:
```
T1: loop { wait(X); print A; signal(Y); }
T2: loop { wait(Y); print B; signal(Z); }
T3: loop { wait(Z); print C; signal(X); }
```
Which initial values make the output ABCABCABC...?

Two threads share binary semaphores s1 = 1 and s2 = 0 (assume s1 is a mutex-like lock):
```
T1: wait(s1); print A; wait(s2); signal(s1); print B;
T2: wait(s1); print C; signal(s2); signal(s1);
```
Which outcomes are possible?

Two threads update shared variables a = 1, b = 1. Each statement is atomic; statements of different threads may interleave in any way.
```
T1: a = a + 1;  b = b + 1;
T2: b = 3 * b;  a = 3 * a;
```
Which set lists exactly the possible final (a, b) pairs?

B is shared and starts at 1. Each statement reads B once or writes it once:
```
P1: C = B + 1;  B = 2 * C;
P2: D = B + 2;  B = 3 * D;
```
How many distinct final values can B take?

All semaphores start at 0, and s is a counting semaphore. Each thread runs once:
```
T1: print A; signal(s); print E;
T2: print B; signal(s);
T3: wait(s); print C; wait(s); print D;
```
Which output strings are possible?

Three threads run once each, and s is a counting semaphore initialised to 0:
```
T1: print A; print B; signal(s);
T2: wait(s); print C;
T3: print D; print E;
```
How many distinct output strings are possible?
